1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:12,560
Remember Smokey the Bear and the commercials that used to be on TV when we were kids?

3
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:20,180
You know, only you can prevent forest fires and how just one careless match could burn down a whole forest.

4
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:35,880
So, if I ever met Smokey, I would ask him, how is it that one careless match can burn down a whole forest, but it takes a whole box of matches to start a campfire?

5
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:38,980
Answer me that, Mr. Smokey Bear.

6
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,879
Hey, this is podcast number 17.

7
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:50,900
Hey!

8
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:17,400
So, welcome back to the Scoutmaster's podcast.

9
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,500
This is Clark Green.

10
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:25,020
On this podcast, in Scoutmastership, in seven minutes or less, we're going to talk about some ways to retain older Scouts.

11
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:26,400
Always an important subject.

12
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:37,060
And then, a historic recording from a little-known radio show about Brick Mason, the Scoutmaster superhero.

13
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:44,480
I don't know if you ever listened to that when you were a kid, but I found some old historic recordings.

14
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:55,000
Later on today, I'm going to be recording an interview with David Scott, who is one of the authors of a new book that's coming out this June called The Scouting Party.

15
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:03,160
And what it is, it's a very thorough historical study of three personalities that were involved in the founding of the BSA.

16
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:08,000
Baden Powell, Ernest Thompson-Seaton, and Dan Beard.

17
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:12,180
And it's a really interesting history.

18
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:23,340
And it's not just a history, but there's a lot of things that we can learn about Scouting and that we can use as we go forward into our next hundred years.

19
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:25,920
David's done a marvelous job of putting this book together.

20
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:29,040
I have an advanced copy of it, and I've been very interested to read it.

21
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:33,420
So, hopefully, that interview will be on the next edition of the Scoutmasters podcast.

22
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:35,900
But there's plenty for us to do today.

23
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:37,560
So, let's get started.

24
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:42,680
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

25
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:56,480
Someday soon, we're going to sit down with the troop and we're going to do a little bit of a membership assessment survey.

26
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:00,580
We're going to see how many scouts we brought in and how many scouts we lost.

27
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:04,700
It's been a particularly active year in that wise.

28
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:12,420
We've brought in about 16 new scouts to a troop of about 35, which is a pretty big bump.

29
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:16,760
And I know of at least five that we're going to lose this year.

30
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:21,200
And the reason that we're losing them is they are too old.

31
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:26,200
Yeah, they're turning 18 and they can't be Boy Scouts anymore.

32
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:28,520
Most of them are headed off to college.

33
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:35,200
A couple might be hanging around the house for a little while, so we might be able to work with them as adult leaders.

34
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:37,160
But it'll be a sad time.

35
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:38,400
So, here's a question.

36
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:41,240
How do we retain older scouts?

37
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:42,280
How do we keep their interest?

38
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:43,980
This is a big deal sometimes.

39
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:50,260
If you put a few scoutmasters in a room, eventually they will start talking.

40
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:52,800
It's a surprise.

41
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:57,920
But the subject will come down to, you know, how do we keep our older boys engaged and interested?

42
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:07,019
How do we keep from losing our 15, 16-year-old scouts to car keys and girlfriends and things like that?

43
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:10,119
A lot of troops do struggle with that.

44
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,339
Not everyone has that problem.

45
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:19,880
Here's what generally, to my mind, keeps older scouts interested.

46
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:23,400
For one, they run their troop.

47
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:29,300
They have real, uncoerced, and independent responsibility for the welfare of the troops program.

48
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:32,980
They own their own triumphs and their own failures.

49
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:42,000
This makes for an endlessly interesting and engaging involvement for a guy who's 15, 16, 17 years old.

50
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:43,900
They really enjoy that.

51
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:50,719
They're encouraged and supported by the adult leadership, but from a respectful distance.

52
00:04:51,719 --> 00:04:52,919
They're not nannied.

53
00:04:53,239 --> 00:04:54,840
They're not helicoptered.

54
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:59,520
The adults accept that mistakes are inevitable.

55
00:05:00,599 --> 00:05:08,219
You have to have a bit of serendipity to understand that, you know, not every mistake is a punishable crime.

56
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:10,739
We're on the side of the boys, right?

57
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:12,219
We're on the side of our scouts.

58
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:15,859
We don't want to punish them for making mistakes.

59
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:17,380
We want to encourage them through them.

60
00:05:18,059 --> 00:05:20,700
You know, you have to let older scouts be teenagers.

61
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:20,860
Teenagers.

62
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:24,340
Now, never at the risk of propriety or safety.

63
00:05:25,159 --> 00:05:30,120
Teenagers do really strange, unpredictable, and downright hilarious things at times.

64
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:32,039
I mean, let's see.

65
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:38,159
Let me see if I can give you a roster of interesting things.

66
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:42,159
Purple hair, green hair, I think red hair.

67
00:05:43,980 --> 00:05:48,680
The affectation of some of the goth, you know, type of stuff.

68
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:55,600
The studded wristbands, the strange necklaces, the funny hats.

69
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:57,880
Believe it or not, a monocle.

70
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:02,800
I don't know where he found it.

71
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:04,860
I had a guy who wore a monocle around.

72
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:07,340
Do you remember being a teenager?

73
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:09,300
They do crazy stuff like that.

74
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:15,600
There's a hundred little quirks, and you might raise an eyebrow to it, and you might wonder.

75
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:20,300
But I'm going to save my energy for more important battles.

76
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:26,100
If a guy shows up and, you know, he wants to express himself in that particular way,

77
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:32,260
I am going to work along with him rather than, you know, tell him to go get his hair cut

78
00:06:32,260 --> 00:06:35,260
or to stop dying at purple or whatever.

79
00:06:35,260 --> 00:06:38,599
So a lot of these things are attitude, right?

80
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:44,740
And not the attitude of the scouts, but the attitude of the adults that are working with them.

81
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:47,580
I mean, you have to be a realist.

82
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:51,380
And if you look at the glass, it should always be half full.

83
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:58,960
We have to accept that a boy of 15, 16 is not going to make every single outing or every single meeting.

84
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:03,659
We need to encourage them to participate in a number of things in their community

85
00:07:03,659 --> 00:07:05,960
and show some interest out there.

86
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:15,620
You know, I will have, oh, maybe one out of four or five guys who all they do is scouts.

87
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:17,400
The other ones are involved in everything.

88
00:07:17,539 --> 00:07:18,340
They're in marching band.

89
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:19,960
They're in AP courses.

90
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:23,460
They're, you know, in the Future Business Leaders of America.

91
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:24,620
They're in the school play.

92
00:07:24,620 --> 00:07:33,520
Well, if I start harping on them about not being at my meetings or on my campouts while they're doing other things,

93
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:36,220
they're going to get disinterested very quickly.

94
00:07:36,660 --> 00:07:38,840
We cooperate with those things in the community.

95
00:07:39,260 --> 00:07:40,920
We don't fight against them.

96
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:42,500
We're not in competition with them.

97
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:48,940
A healthy, strong, vibrant atmosphere of positive peer pressure is good.

98
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:51,860
Positive peer pressure is good.

99
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:56,400
You know, where we celebrate achievements and we minimize shortfalls.

100
00:07:57,620 --> 00:08:01,840
And if that gets out into the troop, that's a fun place to be.

101
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:05,440
It's an encouraging and affirming place to be.

102
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:15,140
A real concern and care for the welfare of younger scouts has really got to be the first priority within a troop.

103
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:22,520
It's got to be something that is promulgated amongst the older boys and it will work.

104
00:08:23,140 --> 00:08:23,940
There's no hazing.

105
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:24,919
There's no cruelty.

106
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:26,020
There's no bullying.

107
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:31,520
It's not tolerated and it's not part of the culture of the troop.

108
00:08:31,659 --> 00:08:33,659
That keeps older boys interested.

109
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:42,659
Scoutmasters and their assistants, we can't nitpick the older scouts' decisions or offer much in the way of unsolicited advice.

110
00:08:42,819 --> 00:08:44,039
It gets tiresome for them.

111
00:08:44,039 --> 00:08:47,160
Older scouts need to have plenty of time to spend together.

112
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:52,860
They'll always value the time they spent working with younger scouts, but they've got to have plenty of time with their peers.

113
00:08:53,199 --> 00:08:59,020
They will find those times and if you can identify them, it's maybe of an evening when you're in camp.

114
00:08:59,319 --> 00:09:03,280
It's at the table with the patrol leader's council when you're not.

115
00:09:03,860 --> 00:09:07,980
They need to be able to associate with guys their own age and they need to work with younger scouts.

116
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:11,400
And there's a balance there and it's not that hard to achieve.

117
00:09:13,220 --> 00:09:21,660
Scouts, both old and young, in a troop that retains older scouts, are made to feel accepted, safe, and happy.

118
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:29,880
These are general, attitudinal, underlying, broad concepts to what keep older boys involved in scouting.

119
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:35,120
They're treated with some respect, even when, you know, they come in with the purple hair.

120
00:09:35,780 --> 00:09:39,160
We have a realistic expectation of their involvement.

121
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:48,640
We create a culture in the troop of concern for our younger scouts and we invest that in the older scouts.

122
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:57,400
They have real responsibility that's not coerced, that's not forced upon them, but it's something that they take upon themselves.

123
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:02,579
And we make sure that everybody feels accepted, safe, and happy.

124
00:10:03,699 --> 00:10:04,959
Older scouts will hang around.

125
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:05,939
They really will.

126
00:10:05,940 --> 00:10:09,280
Try a few of those things and let me know how they work.

127
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:34,600
The time is marked by a lack of truth.

128
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:40,460
The world turns to Rick Mason, Scoutmaster.

129
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:45,880
Ah, and how is the Bison Patrol this fine evening?

130
00:10:46,140 --> 00:10:47,600
Ah, it's Buffalo, isn't it?

131
00:10:47,820 --> 00:10:48,120
Where?

132
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:51,240
No, no, Mr. Mason, that's the name of our patrol.

133
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:52,480
The Buffaloes?

134
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:53,220
Oh.

135
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:54,760
Naturally.

136
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:56,020
Yes.

137
00:10:56,580 --> 00:10:59,740
Hey, hey, don't forget you're having supper with us tonight, right, Mr. M?

138
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:07,980
Ah, the vaporous essences emanating from the melange of meat and vegetables you are preparing has sharply piqued my assorience.

139
00:11:08,780 --> 00:11:10,240
Yeah, okay.

140
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:11,920
Hey, Johnny, how you doing?

141
00:11:11,960 --> 00:11:12,140
Hey.

142
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:13,340
Wow, Mr. M.

143
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:15,300
Hey, doesn't that stew smell great?

144
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:16,400
Precisely.

145
00:11:16,500 --> 00:11:18,200
Jeez, I could eat a horse.

146
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:21,800
Now, do I understand you to exercise a figure of speech?

147
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:22,460
Yeah.

148
00:11:22,780 --> 00:11:27,100
Or to express the intention to consume an actual equine?

149
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:29,920
Hey, fellas, we better build the fire up a little bit, huh?

150
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:32,260
What?

151
00:11:32,260 --> 00:11:48,260
Scouts, I fear I must tell you, you have fundamentally underestimated the volume of fuel required to raise the temperature of your comestibles and thus achieve a state of optimum edibility.

152
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:50,300
What?

153
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:52,760
Get some more sticks.

154
00:11:55,439 --> 00:11:57,700
Hey, you guys, the stew's ready.

155
00:11:58,600 --> 00:11:59,600
Here you go, Mr. M.

156
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:00,400
Ah, thank you.

157
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:02,160
Ouchie, ouchie.

158
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:03,339
You okay there, Mr. M.?

159
00:12:03,339 --> 00:12:04,939
Ouchie, ouchie, ouchie, ouch.

160
00:12:05,660 --> 00:12:14,800
Yes, it seems the aluminum alloy of my mess kit was chosen for its ability to transfer heat to its exterior surface.

161
00:12:15,439 --> 00:12:16,760
Yeah, yeah, you gotta watch out.

162
00:12:16,819 --> 00:12:19,579
The stew was really hot and it was on the fire.

163
00:12:24,579 --> 00:12:31,060
Join us again for another exciting episode in the career of Richard Mason.

164
00:12:31,640 --> 00:12:33,760
king of the scoutmaster.

165
00:12:33,839 --> 00:12:34,219
Okay.

166
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:48,100
Boy Scouts!

167
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:51,980
Boy Scouts!

168
00:12:58,020 --> 00:13:01,220
Write me a letter

169
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:03,520
Send it by mail

170
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:06,600
Email that is, folks.

171
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:10,199
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

172
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:19,840
Here's a recent email I received.

173
00:13:21,079 --> 00:13:26,660
We have an issue in our troop that I think is going to develop into a real dust-up if I don't step in promptly.

174
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:31,360
We have formed an adult patrol to show the Scouts how they should be working together

175
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:35,020
and show them also what they can do with camp cooking.

176
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:40,440
We're not going to do lobster and prime rib, but we're not going to do oatmeal in a bag either.

177
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:45,280
We have many adults that love cooking outside and want to bring all their gadgets to the outing.

178
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:48,260
My committee chair wants us only to use troop equipment,

179
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:55,420
even though many adults, me included, have purchased over the years all sorts of equipment to make our camping experience even more enjoyable.

180
00:13:56,560 --> 00:13:58,000
My question is this.

181
00:13:58,100 --> 00:14:03,319
Is there any guidance from the BSA as to what the adults are allowed to bring on a campout,

182
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:06,200
or is this strictly a troop-by-troop decision?

183
00:14:07,640 --> 00:14:09,960
Well, I'm reading a lot between the lines here,

184
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:16,720
and what I am seeing is I'm seeing a troop where the adults are going to take up two sides of a question,

185
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:19,660
and they're going to start throwing policies back at one another.

186
00:14:20,020 --> 00:14:21,500
I think that's a really bad idea.

187
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:28,940
Instead of arguing this question, why not apply the scouting program to it,

188
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:30,880
and let's see what we come up with.

189
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:38,480
You know, look for the broad concepts of what scouting is to be and see if there's an answer there.

190
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:43,520
So let's start with the direct policy question.

191
00:14:44,540 --> 00:14:48,620
Is there any guidance from the BSA as to what adults are allowed to bring on a campout?

192
00:14:48,620 --> 00:14:57,500
Well, there's some lists in the Scoutmaster handbook of things that are suggested to bring.

193
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:00,460
There's prohibition against bringing certain things.

194
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:05,840
But I don't know of any kind of a policy that you would turn to

195
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:10,200
to kind of make a decision about how elaborate your camp gadgets are going to be.

196
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:16,580
I do know the underlying principle that will help you solve this problem.

197
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:21,400
I'm going to begin by saying patrols are not for adults.

198
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:23,680
Scouting?

199
00:15:25,260 --> 00:15:26,460
It's not for adults.

200
00:15:27,620 --> 00:15:28,740
Scouting is for boys.

201
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:31,320
That's right.

202
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:36,300
Did he just say scouting is not for adults?

203
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:38,500
Yeah, that's what I said.

204
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:46,180
Yeah, we go on backpacking trips and we go on hiking trips and we go on camping trips and things like that.

205
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:51,940
But we have to maintain a respectable distance so that scouts can function as scouts.

206
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:58,900
And that their experience is not colored by a bunch of adults looking over their shoulders all the time.

207
00:15:58,900 --> 00:16:06,300
The main reason they want to do this, so the email says, is to show the scouts how they should be working together

208
00:16:06,300 --> 00:16:09,060
and show them just what they can do with camp cooking.

209
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:11,160
I got news for you.

210
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:13,800
They don't have a whole lot of patience for it.

211
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:18,199
Once in a while you come along and you have a real camp chef who really wants to do stuff up.

212
00:16:18,200 --> 00:16:27,820
But as far as they're concerned, for the most part, if you have a boy living in your household and he's 14 years old, 15 years old,

213
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:31,520
meals are an impediment to doing something else.

214
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:32,500
Am I right?

215
00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:36,920
I mean, you want to get through that as fast as you can and you want to move on.

216
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:37,740
Well, wait a minute.

217
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:40,040
We're supposed to teach them how to cook outside and things like that.

218
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:40,620
Well, of course.

219
00:16:40,620 --> 00:16:48,220
But they're just going to be aggravated by an adult patrol who's constantly trying to show them what they should be doing.

220
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:54,280
They're going to be aggravated by the fact that the adult patrol has a bunch of gear that they can't even hope to have.

221
00:16:55,640 --> 00:17:02,500
And they're going to be aggravated by the fact that adults are transgressing on their playing field and trying to play.

222
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:05,260
I really don't think it's a good idea.

223
00:17:05,260 --> 00:17:15,060
Listen, I know, I know that a lot of troops have, you know, kind of an old goat patrol or a rocking chair patrol and things like that.

224
00:17:15,099 --> 00:17:17,560
And if you look, you can find patches for it and everything like that.

225
00:17:17,819 --> 00:17:28,160
But let's try and decide, answer the question, with the scout program itself, just look at some of the underlying concepts and see if that's a good idea.

226
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:33,100
Now, you would think with scouting being around for 100 years that somebody's tried this out before.

227
00:17:33,100 --> 00:17:41,379
Hey, we'll have an example patrol of adults and that'll show the boys what to do and that that would be a tremendously effective and wonderful way to go about it.

228
00:17:41,959 --> 00:17:43,620
Do you see that anywhere in the program?

229
00:17:44,280 --> 00:17:48,260
Do you see it anywhere over the past 100 years of history with the program?

230
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:49,459
I don't.

231
00:17:49,639 --> 00:17:50,320
I haven't seen it.

232
00:17:50,899 --> 00:17:55,379
I like to have fun and I like to pal around with my fellow adult leaders just as much as anybody.

233
00:17:56,620 --> 00:18:02,719
But we're going to draw the line at kind of stepping onto the field and playing the same game that the scouts are.

234
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:12,760
The response I received to that particular set of thoughts was that I don't know what the right answer is, but you've given me further food for thought.

235
00:18:13,459 --> 00:18:15,919
Man, adults sure can screw up a good thing, no?

236
00:18:17,379 --> 00:18:18,480
Yeah, that's right.

237
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:27,860
Most of my problems as a scoutmaster have come because adults, though good intentioned, nice folks, get the wrong ideas about things sometimes.

238
00:18:27,860 --> 00:18:30,280
And here's what I think the three major things are.

239
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:32,260
They lose sight of the aims of scouting.

240
00:18:33,459 --> 00:18:37,300
They try to make scouting easier or more fun for themselves.

241
00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:43,639
Or they assume scouting is even remotely about their participation in it.

242
00:18:44,719 --> 00:18:44,959
No.

243
00:18:45,199 --> 00:18:46,919
What are we there for?

244
00:18:47,659 --> 00:18:48,620
What are we supposed to do?

245
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:51,959
We're supposed to be there so scouts can go camping.

246
00:18:52,239 --> 00:18:54,780
We help them with administrative things.

247
00:18:55,459 --> 00:18:58,280
We keep the troop records and things like that.

248
00:18:58,780 --> 00:19:01,419
We do whatever they can't do.

249
00:19:01,820 --> 00:19:03,860
We lend the whole thing a little bit of continuity.

250
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:04,860
We do some training.

251
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:05,760
We do some mentoring.

252
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:14,120
You know, frankly, if adults won't go on outings because they can't tow along all their gadgets or be part of a patrol,

253
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:17,060
I'm just as happy to have them stay home.

254
00:19:18,159 --> 00:19:20,020
Now, that might seem a bit strong to you.

255
00:19:20,679 --> 00:19:22,959
I know a troop needs adults in order to operate.

256
00:19:23,840 --> 00:19:28,300
But 80% of the leadership in a well-run troop comes from the scouts.

257
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:32,800
And quite frankly, if you have the wrong idea about scouting, stay home.

258
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:40,919
Now, I've devoted, you know, the greater part of my adult life in a great measure to making sure that scouting works in my troop.

259
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:45,100
And I understand that there is a crucial role for adults to help out.

260
00:19:46,060 --> 00:19:57,660
But at the same time, if we think it's all about us, if we think that we're going to form some kind of idealized patrol that is going to show scouts the way,

261
00:19:58,520 --> 00:20:06,500
then I think we need to go back and we just kind of need to quietly reexamine exactly why we're involved and what the goals of the program are.

262
00:20:07,340 --> 00:20:09,020
And we need to stick with that.

263
00:20:09,020 --> 00:20:10,620
Let's put our energy into that.

264
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:13,600
Do you want to bring along your gadgets?

265
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:14,880
I really don't have a problem.

266
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:16,440
I don't have a problem with that.

267
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:19,980
But don't do it as an example for boys about what they should be doing.

268
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:22,240
Let them find their own way.

269
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:25,000
Let them figure things out.

270
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:26,440
Mentor.

271
00:20:27,620 --> 00:20:27,980
Guide.

272
00:20:28,660 --> 00:20:29,400
Help them discover.

273
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:34,660
It's just a much more satisfying way of being involved in scouting as I've told you.

274
00:20:34,660 --> 00:21:01,320
Thank you for listening to this edition of the Scoutmasters podcast.

